# A semicolon joins two clauses that could each stand as a sentence,
# and the reader has to find the seam between them. A period puts the
# seam where the reader expects one. The rule flags a word with a
# semicolon glued to it.
#
# A closing paren before the mark passes, so a call in a comment
# (`foo();`) stays quiet. A loop header in a source comment (`for (i =
# 0; i < n; i++)`) still fires, which is why the rule sits at
# Recommended. Harper does not tag a digit glued to a semicolon as a
# number, so `5;` passes either way and NUM stays out of the class.
#
# Splitting a contrastive negation (`This isn't a cache; it's a
# ledger`) at the semicolon silences ProseContrastiveNegation, which
# reads one sentence at a time, but the denied framing remains. Drop
# the first clause instead of splitting it.
expr main [([NOUN, VERB, ADJ, ADV, PRON, PROPN, DET];)]
let message "Semicolon. End the sentence with a period."
let description "Flags a semicolon after a word, where it joins two clauses that could each stand as a sentence."
let becomes ""
let kind "Punctuation"
allows "Call foo(); the hook returns."
allows "The value is 5; the limit is 10."
